      ⍝ Bugs28 #48 regression: ⎕EX of a NESTED vector of names (e.g.
      ⍝ ⎕EX 'ZZ48XX' 'ZZ48YY', two multi-character names -- unlike
      ⍝ single-CHARACTER names, which strand-collapse into one simple
      ⍝ flat vector and never exercise this code path at all) flattened
      ⍝ the nested value into a single garbage name via
      ⍝ UCS_string_vector's get_char_value() calls on PointerCell
      ⍝ items, always returning the scalar 1 (or misreading pointer
      ⍝ bytes as a bogus Unicode code point) regardless of how many
      ⍝ names B actually held, and expunging nothing. Fixed in
      ⍝ QuadFunction.cc's new quad_EX_NC_names(): detect nesting and,
      ⍝ if present, disclose one name per top-level item (fully, not
      ⍝ just one level -- ⊂⊂'ABCDE' nests TWICE, since the first ⊂ of
      ⍝ a genuine (non-scalar) vector isn't a no-op the way ⊂⊂'X' is
      ⍝ for a single-CHARACTER 'X') instead of flattening B outright.
      ⍝
      ZZ48XX←1 ⋄ ZZ48YY←2
      ⎕NC 'ZZ48XX' 'ZZ48YY'
2 2

      ⎕EX 'ZZ48XX' 'ZZ48YY'
1 1

      ⎕NC 'ZZ48XX' 'ZZ48YY'
0 0

      ⍝ control: a scalar B (however deeply self-enclosed) still gives
      ⍝ a scalar result
      ⍝
      ZZ48X←9
      ⎕EX ⊂⊂'ZZ48X'
1

      ⎕NC 'ZZ48X'
0

      ⍝ regression (Bugs28 #88): same fix as ⎕NC (see Quad_NC.tc) -- a
      ⍝ 0-row character matrix B used to give ⎕EX a scalar Z with its
      ⍝ one cell never written, instead of a 0-element vector.
      ⍝
      ⍴⎕EX 0 3⍴'A'
0

